WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.740
How do we find the inverse of Matrix A here?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.420
It's a 3X3 matrix this time.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.060
The formula for A inverse that we saw in the previous lesson

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.240
is valid for all square Matrices.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.400
It's equal to one over the determinant of 'A',

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.820
multiplied by the adjoin of 'A'.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.460
No elementary operations needed!

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.380
The condition states that the determinant of 'A' must not be equal to zero.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.560
That's because the denominator cannot be equal to zero.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.820
If the determinant of 'A' is zero,

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.780
then we can say that the inverse of Matrix A does not exist.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.600
So that's the first thing we should do. We find the determinant of 'A'.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.380
We can have the first row as the reference,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.900
because we've already solved innumerable determinant problems,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.740
let's go through this quickly.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.440
The Element two will be multiplied with this determinant to get this.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.840
Two multiplied with 1 times 0, minus 2 times 1.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.660
The next element is 'minus 1', we get this.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.160
Minus because we are multiplying this product with 'minus 1' raised to 1 plus 2.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.060
The last element for the first row is 0.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.720
Because it's 0 it doesn't matter what it's multiplied with.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.600
The product will always be 0.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.900
This will be the value of the determinant.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:30.480
This part in the brackets will equal 0 minus 2. Which equals 'minus 2.'

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.920
2 times 'minus 2' will equal minus 4.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.220
What about the second product?

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.080
Inside the brackets we have '0 minus 2' which equals 'minus 2.'

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.100
1 times 'minus 2', is 'minus 2.' And this product is 0.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.860
'Minus 4' 'minus 2', will equal 'minus 6'.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.040
We have the determinant now.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.580
It's 'minus 6.' As it's not equal to 0

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.220
we can say that it's non singular.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.940
The inverse of this Matrix exists.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.600
So we can move on to finding the adjoint of A now.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.100
To find the adjoint we first find the matrix of co-factors of each of the elements,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.920
and then find the transpose of that Matrix.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.900
So let's find the co-factors of each of the elements now.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.820
The first element is two.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.740
Its co-factor will equal 'minus 1' raised to 1 plus 1, times its minor.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.840
Its minor is the determinant of what's left

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.460
after excluding its row and column.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.500
So the co-factor will equal 1 multiplied by 0 minus 2, 'minus 2.'

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.840
The co-factor of this 2, is 'minus 2.'

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.060
Now we come to the co-factor of 'minus 1.' It will equal

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:50.800
'minus 1' raised to 1 Plus 2, times 0, times 0 minus 2, times 1.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.400
'Minus 1' times 'minus 2' is 2.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:58.300
Similarly we get the co factor of 0 as 'minus 1.'

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.460
Now please pause the video take your time

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.140
and solve for the remaining 6 co- factors.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.220
These are the six remaining co-factors.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.340
This transpose will give us the adjoint of Matrix A.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.440
To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and the columns.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.300
The first row will become the first column

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.020
'minus 2', 2, 'minus 1.'

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.680
The second row will be the second column.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.300
0, 0, 'minus 3'.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.980
And lastly the third Row will become the third column.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.900
'minus 2', 'Minus 4', 2.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.520
Now we have the adjoint as well.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.500
And because we have both we can find A inverse now.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.400
Let's write it here.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.900
1 over determinant of A will be 1 over 'minus 6.'

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.780
We multiply this with the adjoint of 'A'.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.980
What will this be equal to?

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.860
This is the adjoint. To get this

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.900
we multiply this scalier with each element of the adjoint.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.980
1 over 'minus 6' times 'minus 2', will equal 1 over 3.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.820
1 over 'minus 6' times 0, will equal 0.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:17.200
1 over 'minus 6' times 'minus 2' will equal 1 over 3 again.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.540
And like always, I want you to find the remaining six elements.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.740
Remember, we need to multiply each of the elements with this scalar.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.360
These are the six elements you should have got.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.820
This is 'A' inverse. We found it using this formula.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.820
So this was pretty long,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.880
but still faster than using an elementary operation.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.860
Remember the formula.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.980
'A' inverse is equal to 1 over the determinant of 'A' times the adjoint of 'A'.

